    "content": "Francia (or the Frankish Kingdom) was the largest post-Roman Germanic kingdom in Western Europe, established by the Franks in 481 AD. Founded by Clovis I, it expanded from Roman Gaul to become a major Christian power, eventually dividing into West Francia (modern France) and East Francia (modern Germany) following the split of Charlemagne's empire. This video explains the history of the Kingdom of West Francia:09:59TimelessTreasuryYouTube• Nov 24, 2023 09:59 History and Evolution Establishment: Established in 481 AD in the former Roman province of Gaul, quickly becoming the dominant power in Western Europe. Key Leaders: Clovis I (united the Frankish tribes), Charlemagne (expanded the empire). Division: The empire was divided in 843 AD among Charlemagne's grandsons into West Francia (western France), East Francia (Germany), and Middle Francia. Conflicts: Faced significant Viking raids, including the siege of Paris in 845 AD, and internal power struggles. Culture and Religion Religion: Shifted from Germanic paganism to Chalcedonian Christianity following the conversion of Clovis I in 509 AD. Culture: Initially dominated by Frankish (Germanic) traditions, it evolved into a blended \"Francien\" (French) culture over time. Language: Developed from Germanic dialects into early French as the Latin/Roman influences merged with Frankish customs. Military and Geography Military: Characterized by \"war band\" structures, where kings were primarily commanders who led battles. They were known for heavy infantry and the eventual development of feudal knighthood. Geography: At its peak, it stretched across modern France, Belgium, Netherlands, Switzerland, and western Germany. Modern-Day Equivalent West Francia evolved into the modern nation of France. East Francia developed into the modern nation of Germany. Middle Francia was divided among its neighbors, with parts forming Italy, Switzerland, and parts of France and Germany. Key Historical Facts Founder: Clovis I First Capital: Tournai (now in Belgium) Long-term Capital: Paris Religion: Germanic paganism →right arrow→ Chalcedonian Christianity Culture: Germanic-Roman blended culture",
